I have a project called "ipsak" that is a "swiss-army knife" for networking.  It is
written in Python, and I'd like to convert it to go, and enhance it significantly.  The
original project is at https://github.com/linsomniac/ipsak to get an idea of what it
does.  I think the current default tool (`ipsak 8.8.8.8`) should be switched to the
sub-command `info`.  I'd like it to be structured so that it can run things in parallel,
like the info command is going to be looking up information from various sources and it
would be nice if they could run as quickly as possible by running multiple requests in
parallel.

I'd also like to start a `ping` tool that is like the normal ping tool, but instead of
printing a line for every packet it provides an updating status line and keeps things
on one line, except when there is packet loss.  In the case of loss, it should print out a
line indicating the start of the loss, and then update once packets start coming back
again, saying something like "Lost 8 packets, ids 7-15", something like that.  It would be
nice if you could update that previous line while the packet loss is happening.  Start a
new line when a new loss event happens.  Some enhanced terminal output handling will
likely be a benefit.

This project should have a nice, joyful to use TUI.
